  "resolution_criteria": "This question will resolve to the total spending by all US Senate candidates running in 2026 according to the [Federal Election Commission](https://www.fec.gov/data/spending-bythenumbers/) (FEC).",
  "fine_print": "This question may wait until December 31, 2026, to ensure that all data is updated and included.",
  "background": "The 2026 midterm elections are scheduled to be held on November 3, 2026. The US Senate has 100 seat seats. [As of early 2025](https://ballotpedia.org/119th_United_States_Congress), 53 are held by members of the Republican Party, 45 are held by Democrats, and 2 are held by independents who [caucus with](https://www.senate.gov/about/glossary.htm#C) the Democrats.\n\nSenators are elected for 6-year terms, and elections are held every two years, such that in each election approximately 1/3 of the seats are up for contest. For 2026, [the 33 seats of \"Class II\" are scheduled for election](https://www.senate.gov/senators/Class_II.htm). As of early 2025, 20 are held by Republicans and 13 of those seats are held by Democrats. The 33 winners of the 2026 Senate elections will join 66 incumbent Senators and the winners of the concurrent House elections to form the 120th Congress.\n\nThe [financing of electoral campaigns](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campaign_finance_in_the_United_States) in the United States happens at the federal, state, and local levels by contributions from individuals, corporations, and political action committees. According to the [Federal Election Commission](https://www.fec.gov/data/spending-bythenumbers/) (FEC) the total amount spent has been:\n\n| Year | Total spending by all Senate candidates running that year |   |\n| ---- | --------------------------------------------------------- | - |\n| 2024 | \\$1,687,323,291                                           |   |\n| 2022 | \\$1,708,197,866                                           |   |\n| 2020 | £2,007,613,859                                            |   |\n\nAs at mid-August 2026, a total of \\$666,336,000 (\\$393,934,664 Democratic, \\$261,005,865 Republican, and \\$11,395,471 Other) has been spent by Senate candidates.",
