  "comments": [
    {
      "author": "DeRuina",
      "created_at": "2026-09-30",
      "text": "The \"most expensive Senate cycle ever\" headlines are worth discounting here, because the resolution source doesn't count most of that money. The FEC dashboard only counts candidate committees, and AdImpact's \\$3.4B projection is mostly super PAC and party money that never touches those totals. Looking at what does count: at the June 30 snapshot, Senate candidate disbursements were about \\$711M in 2022 and \\$682M in 2024, versus roughly \\$670M this cycle per the live FEC API. Both prior cycles finished around 2.5x their June figure. So the candidate-side pace is running slightly behind, not ahead, even with Talarico and Ossoff burning tens of millions on ads. The June NRSC v. FEC ruling could push more spending through party committees rather than candidates, which would widen that gap.\n\n\n\nTwo things to watch: the October 15 Q3 filings (if Senate disbursements through September 30 come in above \\~\\$1.05B, the 2.5x pattern is holding), and whether the resolver waits for the December 3 post-general reports, which fall a day after this question closes. Without them, the dashboard would only reflect spending through mid-October and could be \\$200–300M short of the eventual total.",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "DaAdCh",
      "created_at": "2026-09-28",
      "text": "@(Theomachado) Thanks for raising this. The purpose and spirit of this question was certainly to capture all relevant financial reporting for the election cycle. So any notable reporting lag (as you mention) should and will be considered when @(admins) determine the final resolution (and exact date). The only limitation is the window of the tournament, but it appears that we have almost a month to wait for the FEC website to be updated (and so will include post-general reports). Please note this clarification, @(moderators) @(predictors).",
      "prediction": null
    },
    {
      "author": "Theomachado",
      "created_at": "2026-09-28",
      "text": "@(DaAdCh) Quick clarification request, because it changes the whole mechanism of this question.\n\nThe FEC page only shows reports already filed. The chart below shows how much of each past cycle's final total was on the page on a given day. There's normally a big step on Dec 3, when the post-general reports (covering Oct 15 – Nov 23) come in.\n\n![](https://cdn.metaculus.com/user_uploaded/Screenshot_2026-09-28_at_3.49.37PM.png)\n\nSo the resolution date decides which reports count:\n\\- Resolved on the Dec 2 page (as in the schedule resolution date): data through Oct 14 only.\n\\- Resolved on a later page as mentioned in the fine print(e.g., Dec 31): post-general reports included.\n\nI am estimating a \\~\\$220M gap from timing alone, bigger than anything the actual campaigns will do. And it moves the right answer by roughly 20%.\n\nCould you confirm which one counts?\n\nSchedule Resolution:![](https://cdn.metaculus.com/user_uploaded/Screenshot_2026-09-28_at_3.50.13PM.png)Fine print: This question may wait until December 31, 2026, to ensure that all data is updated and included.",
      "prediction": null
    }
  ],
